      "Almost every piece of advice about your phone is a scolding with a step count attached. Try harder. Want it more. Be the sort of person who simply does not pick it up.",
      "It has never worked for anybody, and the reason is not mysterious. You are not in a fair fight. Treating a lost fight as a character flaw just adds shame on top of the exhaustion, and shame has never once made anybody better at concentrating.",
      "## Work out the bill before you try to fix anything",
      "The usual mistake is jumping straight to solutions. New app, new rule, new system, quietly abandoned by Thursday.",
      "Do the accounting first, and do it properly \u2014 not a vague sense that you are on your phone too much. Itemised. What did this actually cost you in the last seven days?",
      "Hours is the obvious line and the least interesting one. The expensive items are the ones you never invoice yourself for. The conversation you were physically present at and can remember nothing about. The book you have restarted four times. That specific flavour of tiredness that comes from a day where you were busy without interruption and produced nothing you could point at.",
      "There are two reasons to do this before anything else. You cannot get properly motivated about a problem you have only felt vaguely. And without a starting number, you will never know whether the thing you changed afterwards actually did anything.",
      "## Notifications are not neutral",
      "A notification is not information. It is a request, sent by somebody with a commercial interest, at a moment they chose rather than one you did.",
