      "Nobody wants to think about this, which is precisely why almost nobody has handled it.",
      "## Compliance is a seat belt",
      "Founders love the marketing side. Growth tactics, positioning, clever pricing. Legal structure feels like a chain that restricts movement, so it gets pushed to next quarter permanently.",
      "But compliance is a seat belt. You do not wear one because you plan to drive into a wall. You wear one because you cannot control the other drivers — the competitor, the disgruntled contractor, the customer who decides to sue.",
      "The bill for skipping it is entirely preventable, and it always arrives at the worst possible moment.",
      "## The corporate veil is not automatic",
      "The foundational rule is a permanent separation between you the person and the company the entity. Operate as a sole proprietor and there is no separation at all — you and the business are legally the same thing.",
      "There is a case in the book about an e-commerce founder doing two million a year in revenue who never got round to filing. A customer sued over an allergic reaction that turned out to be caused by their own pre-existing condition. Being right did not help. Defence costs hit a hundred and fifty thousand, and with no entity in the way, the plaintiff's lawyers went straight at his personal account, his family savings and his house. He settled and lost everything.",
      "Filing the formation documents is not the end of it either. Neglect the entity and a judge will pierce the veil, remove the protection entirely, and expose your personal assets anyway. Three things keep it standing:",
      "**Never mix the money.** Company card for personal groceries, a streaming subscription, a mortgage payment, a pack of gum — every one is a crack. In litigation, opposing counsel subpoenas the bank records specifically hunting for a pattern of personal use, because that pattern lets them argue the company is just you wearing a hat.",
      "**Keep the formalities.** Document decisions, hold the annual meeting even if it is a meeting with yourself, file state reports on time, keep signed minutes. The law grants solo operators no exemption. A company of one still needs a paper trail showing the company thinks separately from the person.",
      "**Capitalise it properly.** Put a hundred dollars into the account and take on half a million in debt and you have proved to a court that the entity was a shell that was never meant to stand on its own.",
      "## Choosing the structure",
      "This is a question of matching how you actually operate to how you want to be taxed.",
      "An **LLC** gives you pass-through taxation. The entity pays no federal income tax; profit flows onto your personal return, and you avoid being taxed twice.",
      "A **C Corporation** is an eighteen-wheeler. Absurd for fetching groceries, necessary if you are hauling venture capital — investors require one because they need clean cap tables and share classes with different voting rights. The cost is double taxation: the company pays corporate tax on profit, then shareholders pay again on dividends.",
      "An **S Corporation** election suits profitable service businesses. Instead of self-employment tax on every dollar of profit, you pay yourself a reasonable salary and take the rest as a distribution, which is not subject to self-employment tax. The saving is real and it compounds annually.",
      "The trap is obvious once stated: pay yourself an artificially tiny salary against a large distribution and you are inviting an audit, reclassification, back taxes and penalties. Reasonable means reasonable.",
      "## The business prenup",
      "Handshakes and goodwill do not survive contact with grief and money. A buy-sell agreement has to be negotiated while everyone still likes each other, and it needs four things.",
      "**Triggering events.** Death, permanent disability, someone wanting out, and divorce. Skip the divorce provision and a family court judge can hand company equity to your partner's ex — putting you in business with someone who has every reason to dislike you.",
      "**A valuation formula.** Agree the maths in advance — a multiple of trailing earnings, or a named third-party appraiser. Anything to remove subjective argument at the exact moment nobody is thinking clearly.",
      "**A way to actually pay for it.** Almost no business has the cash sitting there to buy out a dead partner's heirs. Company-owned term life policies on each partner fund that instantly, without touching working capital. For departures that are not deaths, define an instalment plan paid out of future earnings.",
      "**Right of first refusal.** A departing partner has to offer their shares to the existing owners first, at the agreed valuation, inside a fixed window. Without it, a competitor or an opportunistic buyer can simply purchase their way onto your cap table.",
      "## The assets you cannot see",
      "Failing to secure intellectual property can erase a company overnight, and the two most common failures are quiet ones.",
      "**The name.** A startup spends real money on a logo, prints the cards, buys the domains, builds traction — then receives a cease-and-desist from a business in another state that registered that exact name federally a decade earlier. Thirty days to scrap everything and rebrand. Search the trademark database for phonetic and same-industry conflicts before you fall in love with a name, not after.",
      "**The logo you paid for.** Paying a designer does not mean you own the work. The creator keeps the rights unless the contract contains an explicit work-for-hire clause and a full assignment of intellectual property. Without that specific language you are licensing your own brand from the person who drew it.",
      "One more worth knowing: a patent is a public document. Filing one means publishing the exact schematics for competitors to study and design around. Sometimes a trade secret — compartmentalised knowledge and real non-disclosure agreements — protects you better than a patent ever could.",
      "## Do this one thing this week",
      "Pull up every freelance and contractor agreement your business has ever signed. Search each one for the words work for hire.",
      "If a contract that produced your logo, your site, your app or your copy does not contain that clause and a clear assignment of rights, you do not own the thing you paid for. Fix it now, while the relationship is good and the person will happily sign an assignment, rather than during an acquisition when their lawyer suddenly understands the leverage.",
      "One note, and it is not a formality: this is the shape of the problem, not advice for your specific situation. Company structure and tax elections turn on details — your state, your income, whether you have employees, what you intend to do in three years. The point of a chapter like this is to tell you which questions to take to an attorney and an accountant, so that the hour you pay for is spent well.",
      "This is one idea out of seventeen chapters on building, running, and growing a business — unit economics, market sizing, sales systems, hiring, and the automation math most people never actually run. If the numbers side is where you keep getting caught out, the whole thing is in the book.",
